 the maxima of experimental 𝜎𝑦, i.e. 𝜎𝑚(0.002) in single-mode (on average) PC samples (see, e.g., Refs. 37,38), we find that 𝜎𝑚(0.002)(α-Fe) ≈ 2.6±0,1 GPa, 𝜎𝑚(0.002)(Ni) ≈1.6±0,1GPa, 𝜎𝑚(0.002) (Cu) ≈1.0±0,1 GPa coincide approximately with the theoretical maxima, with allowance for the various definitions of HP coefficients according to the literature (see Table 2 in Ref. 36 with 𝑘(Cu) ∈[0.01,0.024] for UFG PC samples). 5. DISCUSSION. For a joint analytical description of the normal and anomalous HP laws for yield strength in the PC (metallic) aggregates with quasi equilibrium defect structure (EDS) it is proposed the model of the single-mode one-phase PC aggregate with EDS and it is developed the statistical approach to deduce the stationary ESDD ρ(b1,…,bk,d,T) established in the PC sample after series of the same SPD tests. The model includes the following natural assumptions: 1) EDS for given T is characterized by single dislocation ensemble with the least Burgers vector b=b1, which corresponds to the equidistant discrete energy spectrum 𝐸𝑑 0, 𝐸𝑑 1,…,𝐸𝑑 𝑛,…, 𝐸𝑑 𝑁 of each grain for any value of average diameter 𝑑 within [10-8-10-2] m; 2) Boltzmann distribution (8): {exp(−𝑛𝑀/𝑁}, n=1,2,… for origin of respective dislocation (of the length nb) with energy scale 𝑀= 1 2 𝐺𝑏3 𝑘𝐵𝑇 ⁄ and 𝑁= 𝑑/𝑏; 3) isotropic orientation of GB with closely packed grains. As the result, the energy 〈𝐸𝑑〉 (9) of an average dislocation in arbitrary grain of PC sample in question correctly describes the behavior of the set of dislocations for CG, NC and subNC samples (11) in accordance with thermo-fluctuation mechanism within SPD. The equilibrium SDD ρ(b,d,T) (13), (14) satisfies by construction to the Conrad expression (14) in the CG limit. For the quasi-static loading (by tension) the t-(equally ε-) evolution of ESDD ρε=ρ(b,d,T;ε) is naturally based on the above assumptions for the model of PC sample enlarged by the system of ε-dependence condition (15), providing a homogeneous changing for probabilities { 𝑃(𝐸𝑛, 𝜀)} with the partial solutions (16) {exp(−𝑛𝑀(𝜀)/𝑁} for effective energy scale 𝑀(𝜀) = 1 2 𝐺𝑏𝜀 3 𝑘𝐵𝑇 ⁄ and Burgers vector 𝑏𝜀= 𝑏(1 + 𝜀). Note, the stationary ESDD is measurable and can be explored to determine yield strength σy, whereas the pure ε-dependent part of ESDD Δ𝜀𝜌 in (19) was derived under evaluation of a number of dislocation N0(ε) in an arbitrary grain with help of the notion of a minimal time ∆t0 (17) of PD for changing of a grain (and then PC sample) along the loading direction on a quantity proportional to a module of the least Burgers vector b. It has permitted to derive the flow stress 𝜎(𝜀) = 𝜎(𝑏, 𝑑, 𝑇; 𝜀) (23) for equilibrium PC sample under assumption of Taylor deformation (dislocation) hardening law validity in all grain size regions. The generalization for the integral FS 𝜎Σ in case of general form of GB disorientation (αi(ε), Ni), i=1,…,k with angles αi(ε) is given by (24) in approximation of additive input of each FS σ𝑖(ε𝑖) with the weight Ni. 16 ALEXANDER RESHETNYAK The expressions for the yield strength 𝜎𝑦(𝑑) = 𝜎(0.002) and 𝜎Σ𝑦(𝑑) = 𝜎Σ(0.002) from the FS 𝜎(𝜀) (23) and 𝜎Σ(𝜀) (24) present the basic results of the statistical approach application. We stress, first, that as compared to 𝜎𝑦(𝑑), [for whose determination according to (22), (23) one can also use the stationary ESDD (13)] the derivation of FS 𝜎(𝜀) (23) and 𝜎Σ(𝜀) (24) depends on a choice of ε-dependent form for probabilities { 𝑃(𝐸𝑛, 𝜀)}, i.e. of the factors 𝑓𝑛(𝜀) (15) and requires further study. Second, the generalized HP relation 𝜎𝑦(𝑑) and FS 𝜎(𝜀) depend on the polyhedral parameter 0 m , (see remark 3)), which may be independently determined both from generalized HP law in the CG (with help of HP coefficient (28)), NC limits as 𝑚̅0 𝐶𝐺, 𝑚̅0 𝑁𝐶 and from asymptotic behavior of ESDD in the same limits as 𝑚0 𝐶𝐺, 𝑚0 𝑁𝐶 then compared as 𝑚̅0 𝐶𝐺= 𝑚̅0 𝑁𝐶, 𝑚0 𝐶𝐺= 𝑚0 𝑁𝐶 (as well as it provides the check of Taylor hardening law application (𝑚̅0 𝐶𝐺, 𝑚̅0 𝑁𝐶) = (𝑚0 𝐶𝐺, 𝑚0 𝑁𝐶)). Third, the sets of the experimental data, e.g. σ0, HP coefficient 𝑘, yield strength σy for the same single-mode PC samples of number P for given T, but with different defect structure characterized by respective GB disorientation (𝛼𝑖𝑠𝑠(0.002), 𝑁𝑖𝑠𝑠), s=1,..,P, 𝑖𝑠=1,…, 𝑘𝑠, and by possible (nano)-porous part from the weak GB phase presence are differed. Thus, to determine parameter 𝑚0 in the framework of the model (23) for pure crystalline phase in CG limit by the rule (28) one should to ordered the average angles 〈𝛼𝑠〉 for each PC sample as 〈𝛼1〉≤〈𝛼2〉≤⋯≤〈𝛼𝑃〉 and to match the values of parameter 𝑚̅01 𝐶𝐺≥𝑚̅02 𝐶𝐺≥… ≥𝑚̅0𝑃 𝐶𝐺. Obtained dependence permitted to reveal the nature of the normal and anomalous HP laws for σy, to determine within the model approximation the extremal grain size 𝑑0 (26) and maximal value σm= max σy=σy(d0) (27) for equilibrium single-mode PC sample. The value 𝑑0(𝜀, 𝑇) decreases in temperatures: 𝑑0(𝜀, 𝑇1) > 𝑑0(𝜀, 𝑇2) for 𝑇1 < 𝑇2, thus determi- ning in part a temperature-dimension effect [30,35] in PC materials. Note, that the latter property is opposite to one marked by item (2) obtained within molecular dynamics si- mulations [19]. In addition, 𝑑0(𝜀, 𝑇) provides increasing in strain: 𝑑0(𝜀1,𝑇) 𝑑0(𝜀2,𝑇) = [ 1+𝜀1 1+𝜀2] 𝑚 ≥1 for 𝜀1 > 𝜀2, which is the basis for experimental finding of 𝑚 to be equal to, 𝑚= 3, 2,1 or 0, thus revealing ε-dependence for 𝑃(𝐸𝑛, 𝜀), (i.e. for 𝑀𝑚(𝜀) = (1 + 𝜀)𝑚1 2𝐺𝑏3 𝑘𝐵𝑇 ⁄ ). The graphical representations (Fig. 5) of the established HP law for single-mode one- phase PC aggregates with BCC (α-Fe), FCC (Cu, Al, Ni) and HCP (α-Ti, Zr) crystal lattices with closely packed grains at T=300 K show a sufficient close coincidence between the theoretical and experimental data for both the extremal grains 𝑑0(0.002,300) and the maximum 𝜎𝑚(0.002) for these materials. (with some specific for the latter in view of not accounting for peculiar features of BCC. FCC, HCP CL for equilibrium PC aggregates, explicit GB regions with weak phase, considered in Refs. 30. 39, excitation at SPD of other dislocation ensembles, twinners). The proposed approach permits the basis for generalization to the case of multi-mode (one-phase) equilibrium PC samples with closely packed grains, arbitrary GB disori- entation, weak GB (with porous) part, and aspects of dispersion. We stress, that the ex- perimental verification of the temperature-dimension effect, which includes T, ε-behavi- or of the extremal grain 𝑑0(𝜀, 𝑇) and 𝜎𝑦, 𝜎𝑚 for PC sample with EDS are very necessary. Acknowledgements The author is grateful to I.L.Buchbinder, Yu.Sharkeev,V.G. Bagrov,S.G.Psakhie,E.Shilko for support and discussions. The work has been supported under the Program of fundamental research at the State Academy of Sciences for 2013-2020. Statistical Quantized Approach to Flow Stress and Generalized HP Law for Deformable Policrystalline Materials 17 REFERENCES 1. Valiev R.Z., Zhilyaev A.P., Langdon T.G., 2014, Bulk Nanostructured Materials: Fundamentals and Applications, Wiley&Sons, New Jersey, 440p. 2. Hall E., 1951, The Deformation and Ageing of Mild Steel, Proc. Roy. Soc. B 64(1), pp.747-753. 3. Petch N.J., 1953, The Cleavage strength of Polycrystals, J. Iron Steel Inst. 174, pp.25-28. 4. Firstov S.A., Lugovskoi Yu.F., Movchan B.A., 2016, Structure, strength and fatigue resistance of microcrystalline and micro-layer materials, Naukova Dumka, Kiev, on Russian, 152p. 5. Glezer A.M., Kozlov E.V., Koneva N.A., et al, 2017, Plastic Deformation of Nanostructured Materials, CRC Press. 334p. 6. Zhu Y.T., Huang J., 2002, Properties and nanostructures of materials processed by SPD technologies, in Ultrafine Grained Materials II, The Minerals, Metals & Materials Society, Pennsylvania, pp.331-340. 7. Masumura R.A., Hazzleddine P.M., Pande C.S., 1998, Yield stress of fine-grained materials, Acta Mater. 46(13), pp.4527-4534. 8. Koneva N.A., Zhukovskii S.P., Lapsker I.A. et al., 1989, in Physics of defects of the surface layers of ma- terials, ed. Romanov A.E., Lenin