14,Ying P; Gao X; Natan A; Urbakh M; Hod O,"Ying, Penghua; Gao, Xiang; Natan, Amir; Urbakh, Michael; Hod, Oded",,Chemifriction and Superlubricity: Friends or Foes?,2025,J. Phys. Chem. Lett.,16,,2934,,,,10.1021/acs.jpclett.5c00193,https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.03078v1,"The mechanisms underlying chemifriction, i.e. the contribution of interfacial bonding to friction in defected twisted graphene interfaces are revealed using fully atomistic machine-learning molecular dynamics simulations. This involves stochastic events of consecutive bond formation and rupture, that are spatially separated but not necessarily independent. A unique shear-induced interlayer atomic transfer healing mechanism is discovered that can be harnessed to design a run-in procedure to restore superlubric sliding. This mechanism should be manifested as negative differential friction coefficients that are expected to emerge under moderate normal loads. A physically motivated phenomenological model is developed to predict the effects of chemifriction in experimentally relevant sliding velocity regimes. This allows us to identify a distinct transition between logarithmic increase and logarithmic decrease of frictional stress with increasing sliding velocity. While demonstrated for homogeneous graphene interfaces, a similar mechanism is expected to occur in other homogeneous or heterogeneous defected two-dimensional material interfaces.",,frictional stress; molecular dynamics; polycrystalline,Article,Final,All Open Access,arXiv,arxiv-2411.03078v1,"1 ChemifricƟon and Superlubricity: Friends or Foes? Penghua Ying1, Xiang Gao1, Amir Natan,2 Michael Urbakh1, Oded Hod1 1 Department of Physical Chemistry, School of Chemistry, The Raymond and Beverly Sackler Faculty of Exact Sciences and The Sackler Center for ComputaƟonal Molecular and Materials Science, Tel Aviv University, Tel Aviv 6997801, Israel 2 Department of Physical Electronics, Tel-Aviv University, Tel-Aviv, 6997801, Israel Abstract: The mechanisms underlying chemifricƟon, i.e. the contribuƟon of interfacial bonding to fricƟon in defected twisted graphene interfaces are revealed using fully atomisƟc machine-learning molecular dynamics simulaƟons. This involves stochasƟc events of consecuƟve bond formaƟon and rupture, that are spaƟally separated but not necessarily independent. A unique shear-induced interlayer atomic transfer healing mechanism is discovered that can be harnessed to design a run-in procedure to restore superlubric sliding. This mechanism should be manifested as negaƟve diﬀerenƟal fricƟon coeﬃcients that are expected to emerge under moderate normal loads. A physically moƟvated phenomenological model is developed to predict the eﬀects of chemifricƟon in experimentally relevant sliding velocity regimes. This allows us to idenƟfy a disƟnct transiƟon between logarithmic increase and logarithmic decrease of fricƟonal stress with increasing sliding velocity. While demonstrated for homogeneous graphene interfaces, a similar mechanism is expected to occur in other homogeneous or heterogeneous defected two-dimensional material interfaces. 2 IntroducƟon Sliding at the interface of two rigid, ﬂat, and weakly interacƟng crystalline surfaces that are stacked in an incommensurate conﬁguraƟon, leads to ultra-low fricƟon (1, 2). This is due to the eﬀecƟve cancellaƟon of lateral forces opposing translaƟonal moƟon — a hallmark of a phenomenon referred to as structural superlubricity (3-8). Van der Waals (vdW) interfaces of layered contacts are ideal plaƞorms to observe superlubricity, where interfacial incommensurability is facilitated by, e.g., the misﬁt angle in twisted graphiƟc interfaces (9, 10), curvature eﬀects in mulƟwalled carbon nanotubes (11), intrinsic laƫce mismatch in graphene/hexagonal boron nitride heterostructure (7, 12), and strain engineering in bilayer graphene (13, 14). The scaling up of superlubricity from the nanoscale to macroscopic contacts inevitably involves structural defects (15), such as atomic vacancies (16, 17), steps (18, 19), edges (20, 21), and grain boundaries (22, 23). The impact of such defects on the fricƟon of superlubric sliding interfaces involves two disƟnct contribuƟons: (i) physical contribuƟons from defect-induced out-of-plane corrugaƟon; and (ii) chemical contribuƟons associated with the formaƟon and rupture of interlayer covalent bonds. While the former have been extensively explored over the past decade (24-33), the atomisƟc insights into the laƩer remain much less understood. We recently demonstrated that this challenge can be addressed by machine-learning graph neural network potenƟals. Speciﬁcally, for bilayer defected graphene, we developed such a potenƟal that achieves ab iniƟo level accuracy in simultaneously describing sliding energy corrugaƟon and interlayer bonding dynamics in superlubric layered contacts (34). In the present leƩer, we use this computaƟonal framework to elucidate the phenomenon of kineƟc chemifricƟon — the fricƟon arising due to chemical bonding dynamics. We perform non-equilibrium molecular dynamics (MD) simulaƟons to study the stochasƟc nature of interlayer bonding dynamics in defected twisted bilayer graphene, and the corresponding impact on structural superlubricity of this incommensurate sliding interface. Based on the numerical results we then construct a theoreƟcal approach that allows us to bridge between atomisƟc simulaƟons and experimentally relevant Ɵme- and length-scales. Methods and models Our model system consists of a 9.43° twisted defected graphene bilayer with a single vacancy in each layer (marked as V1V1), encompassing overall 590 atoms (see Fig. 1a). In accordance with experimental observaƟons,(35-38) the single atomic vacancies are taken to be unpassivated in our model system. The two vacancies were posiƟoned to ensure that upon relaƟve sliding of 2.59 nm (half of the box length) between the top and boƩom layers along the zigzag (𝑥-axis) direcƟon they align in an eclipsed conﬁguraƟon. Overall, the laterally periodic V1V1 model has a vacancy fracƟon of approximately 0.3%, comparable to experimental observaƟons (17). 3 The reacƟve sliding dynamics simulaƟons were performed using the recently developed machine learning potenƟal (34), as implemented in the LAMMPS package (39). In these simulaƟons, the top layer is driven laterally along the zigzag direcƟon by a rigid slider (replicaƟng the iniƟal top layer) via a harmonic spring, whereas the boƩom layer atoms are anchored to their iniƟal posiƟons with springs of the same sƟﬀness (see SupporƟng InformaƟon (SI) Fig. S1). Due to the stochasƟc nature of the interlayer binding process (see, e.g., Ref. (40)), a minimum of 50 independent trajectories (each with a diﬀerent random number seed for the iniƟal atomic velocity distribuƟon) was used to evaluate the bond formaƟon probability and calculate the average kineƟc fricƟon. Unless noted otherwise, each trajectory involved a total sliding displacement of 5.18 nm , corresponding to the dimension of the supercell along the zigzag direcƟon. To characterize interlayer bond formaƟon and rupture, we used an interatomic distance criterion of 1.8 Å for atoms residing in diﬀerent layers. More details regarding the reacƟve sliding dynamics simulaƟon setup are provided in SI SecƟon S1. Fig. 1. V1V1 interface sliding dynamics simulaƟons at a temperature of 300 K and a relaƟve sliding velocity of 10 m s ⁄ . (a) Top view of the 9.43° twisted V1V1 bilayer graphene considered in this work. The unsaturated dangling atoms around the vacancies in the top and boƩom layer are highlighted in blue and red, respecƟvely. (b) Shear stress traces of the twisted V1V1 bilayer under external normal loads of 0, 1, 2, and 3 GPa obtained from the atomisƟc MD simulaƟons (solid blue lines) and the developed phenomenological model (Eq. 8, dashed orange lines). (c) A representaƟve trajectory demonstraƟng consecuƟve bond formaƟon and rupture events in three regions (marked by 1-3 and enclosed by verƟcal dashed lines) occurring under a normal load of 3 GPa. The fricƟon trace (boƩom), and corresponding snapshots, showing binding pathways in top (upper row) and front (middle row) views, are presented. For clarity, only atoms in the vicinity of the defects are presented, with the six dangling atoms highlighted in blue (top layer) or red (boƩom layer). AtomisƟc snapshots were visualized using the OVITO package (41). 4 Results We ﬁrst examined the impact of external normal load on the bonding dynamics and sliding fricƟon. Figure 1b displays four representaƟve fricƟon traces obtained at a temperature of 𝑇 = 300 K and a sliding velocity of 𝑣= 10 m s ⁄ , under external normal loads of 𝜎 = 0, 1, 2, and 3 GPa. Similar to previous results (34), we ﬁnd that under zero normal load, no apparent covalent bonding between the two layers occurs even when the defects are eclipsed, leading to a relaƟvely smooth shear trace and low fricƟon. Increasing the load to 1 or 2 GPa results in the formaƟon of covalent bonds, as manifested by the pronounced shear-stress peak appearing at a lateral displacement of 𝑥≈2.75 nm , slightly beyond the eclipsed defects conﬁguraƟon. At a higher normal load to 3 GPa, two addiƟonal shear-stress features appear (at 𝑥≈2.55 and 𝑥≈3.15 nm), corresponding to consecuƟve bond formaƟon and rupture events (see SI Movies S1-S3 for single and mulƟple binding events). These results suggest that, beyond the increase of fricƟon with normal load due to enhanced Pauli repulsions at the prisƟne interface regions, covalent bonding in defected regions introduces a signiﬁcant posiƟve contribuƟon to the kineƟc fricƟon coeﬃcient. Fig. 2. StochasƟc interlayer binding dynamics. Interlayer bond formaƟon probability of various atom pairs in a 9.43° twisted V1V1 bilayer as a funcƟon of (a) external normal load, (b) temperature, and (c) sliding velocity, obtained from ensemble MD simulaƟons (circles) and the kineƟc model (Eqs. 4-7) (solid lines). In the top panels, 𝑝ଵ, 𝑝ଶ, and 𝑝ଷ denote probabiliƟes for the three disƟnct binding regions. The boƩom panels present the overall probability for region 2 and the corresponding contribuƟon of each speciﬁc binding atomic pair. At the chosen sliding conﬁguraƟon, we idenƟﬁed ﬁve disƟnct interlayer bonded pairs, involving the dangling atoms of the defects. To characterize those, we mark the three dangling atoms in top layer as Tଵ, Tଶ, and Tଷ, and corresponding atoms in boƩom layer as Bଵ, Bଶ, and Bଷ. Figure 1c shows a representaƟve trajectory obtained under an external normal load of 3 GPa that incorporates three regions for consecuƟve bond formaƟon and 5 rupture events. The ﬁrst event (""region 1”) occurs at lateral displacement range of 2.40 nm < 𝑥 < 𝑥ଵ ௠௔௫= 2.60 nm featuring binding between the top rightmost (Tଶ) and the boƩom leŌmost (Bଵ ) dangling atoms, which we mark as T2B1. The second event (""region 2”) occurs at a lateral displacement range of 2.60 nm < 𝑥< 𝑥ଶ ௠௔௫= 2.85 nm, featuring a TଶBଶ covalent bond. We note that at this eclipsed conﬁguraƟon, the probabiliƟes of forming TଵBଵ or TଷBଷ bonds are comparable to that of the featured TଶBଶ bond, and the idenƟty of the bond formed is determined stochasƟcally. We further note that we did not idenƟfy any events of simultaneous mulƟple interlayer bond formaƟon in this region over 100 independent trajectories. We aƩribute this to the fact that the simultaneous formaƟon of two or three interlayer bonds in this region involves a signiﬁcantly higher transiƟon energy barrier (TEB) as compared to the single-bond formaƟon (see SI secƟon S2). Finally, the third binding event (""region 3”) occurs at a lateral displacement range of 2.85 nm < 𝑥< 𝑥ଷ ௠௔௫= 3.20 nm, demonstraƟng a TଵBଶ bonding conﬁguraƟon. Bond formaƟon in reacƟve sliding interfaces is a stochasƟc process, inﬂuenced by the external normal load, sliding velocity, and temperature, as validated through MD simulaƟons (see Fig. 2 and SI secƟon S5). In the top panels of Fig. 2 and SI Fig. S4, we present the total probabiliƟes associated with the three disƟnct regions outlined in Fig. 1c (marked as 𝑝ଵ , 𝑝ଶ , and 𝑝ଷ ), whereas in the boƩom panels of the ﬁgure we show the individual bond formaƟon probabiliƟes of the three diﬀerent pairs in region 2 (marked as 𝑝୘భ୆భ , 𝑝୘మ୆మ , and 𝑝୘య୆య ). All bond formaƟon probabiliƟes are found to grow with increasing normal load (see Fig. 2a and SI Fig. S4a), leading to the enhancement of the shear stress peak (see Fig. 1b). At a sliding velocity of 𝑣= 10 m s ⁄ , 𝑝ଶ is much larger than 𝑝ଵ and 𝑝ଷ for all normal load considered, reaching 100% at 2.5 GPa. Speciﬁcally, in region 2, TଶBଶ and TଷBଷ pairs are more likely to bind than TଵBଵ with saturaƟon probabiliƟes of 𝑝୘మ୆మ≈𝑝୘య୆య≈2𝑝୘భ୆భ≈40% . Interlayer bond formaƟon probability increases with temperature (see Fig. 2b). In region 2, characterized by a lower TEB (see SI Fig. S2a), it saturates around room temperature, whereas in regions 1 and 3 a higher saturaƟon temperature is observed. The atomisƟc simulaƟons presented above provide a microscopic understanding of the chemifricƟon mechanisms involving interlayer bond formaƟon and rupture. Nonetheless, they are limited in their ability to reproduce experimentally relevant condiƟons, such as low sliding velociƟes (see Fig. 2c) and long sliding scenarios. To access such parameter regimes, we developed a physically moƟvated phenomenological kineƟc model, derived based on the twisted V1V1 bilayer simulaƟon results. The model describes the chemifricƟon component in terms of the rate of shear-induced interlayer bond formaƟon and its survival probability. The former is given by the Arrhenius law (42-44): 𝑟(𝑥, 𝜎, 𝑇) = 𝑓଴𝑒 ൬ି ಶೌ(ೣ)షಶ೘(഑) ೖా೅ ൰, (1) 6 where 𝑓଴ is the aƩempt frequency, 𝑘୆ is the Boltzmann constant, 𝐸௔(𝑥) is the TEB in the absence of external load, and 𝐸௠(𝜎) is the eﬀecƟve TEB reducƟon due to the applicaƟon of normal pressure, 𝜎. In our model, 𝐸௔(𝑥) is assumed to have a parabolic dependence on the lateral defect displacement (𝑥) , obtaining a minimum (𝐸୫୧୬) at the eclipsed conﬁguraƟon (displacement of 𝑥௘ ): 𝐸௔(𝑥) = 𝐸୫୧୬+ 𝛽(𝑥−𝑥௘)ଶ , where 𝛽 is a posiƟve constant. Furthermore, the TEB reducƟon is assumed to be linear with normal pressure having an acƟvaƟon volume 𝛼, 𝐸௠(𝜎) = 𝛼𝜎 (45-47). To describe the bond formaƟon kineƟcs, we introduce the probability of the system to avoid interlayer bonding, 𝑠(𝑥, 𝜎, 𝑣, 𝑇), up to sliding displacement, 𝑥, at a sliding velocity 𝑣, which is assumed to saƟsfy an irreversible ﬁrst-order rate equaƟon: ௗ௦(௫(௧),ఙ,௩,்) ௗ௧ = ௗ௦(௫(௧),ఙ,௩,்) ௗ௫ 𝑣= −𝑟(𝑥, 𝜎, 𝑇) 𝑠(𝑥, 𝜎, 𝑣, 𝑇). (2) SubsƟtuƟng Eq. 1 into Eq. 2, and solving for 𝑠(𝑥, 𝜎, 𝑣, 𝑇) we obtain: 𝑠(𝑥, 𝜎, 𝑣, 𝑇) = 𝐴𝑒⎝ ⎛ି √ഏ೑బ మೡටഁ/൫ೖా೅൯ ௘ ൬ഀ഑షಶౣ ౟౤ ೖా೅ ൰ ୣ୰୤ቀඥఉ/(௞ా் )(௫ି௫೐)ቁ ⎠ ⎞ , (3) where erf(𝑥) = ଶ √గ∫𝑒ି ௧మ𝑑𝑡 ௫ ଴ is the Gaussian error funcƟon (48) and 𝐴 is a normalizaƟon constant, set to saƟsfy the iniƟal condiƟon 𝑠(𝑥= 0) = 1, where 𝑥= 0 is the interlayer shiŌ at which the lateral distance between the two defects is the largest within our laterally periodic supercell (see Fig.1a). In parƟcular, for region 1, where only a single binding scenario is possible, Eq. 3 yields a bond formaƟon probability of: 𝑝୘మ୆భ(𝜎, 𝑣, 𝑇) = 1 −𝑠ଵ(𝑥ଵ ௠௔௫, 𝜎, 𝑣, 𝑇), (4) where 𝑠ଵ is given by Eq. 3 with 𝐸௠௜௡= 𝐸௠௜௡் మ஻భ being the load-free energy barrier for TଶBଵ bond formaƟon in this region. For region 2, where three potenƟal binding scenarios exist, the rate term of Eq. 1 is wriƩen as a sum of the three possible bond formaƟon rates. This, in turn, yields a total bond formaƟon probability of (see SI secƟon S3 for detailed derivaƟons): 𝑝ଶ(𝜎, 𝑣, 𝑇) = 1 − 𝐴𝑒 ∑ ⎝ ⎜⎜ ⎛ି √ഏ೑బ మೡටഁ/൫ೖా೅൯ ௘ ቌഀ ഑షಶౣ ౟౤ ౐೙ా೙ ೖా೅ ቍ ୣ୰୤ቀඥఉ/(௞ా் )(௫మ೘ೌೣି ௫೐)ቁ ⎠ ⎟⎟ ⎞ య೙సభ , (5) where 𝐸୫୧୬ ୘೙୆೙ is the barrier height associated with the load-free formaƟon of a T௡B௡ bond, with 𝑛= 1, 2, or 3. Using this expression one can approximate the formaƟon probability of individual interlayer pairs via: 𝑝୘೙୆೙= 𝑝ଶ ௥౐೙ా೙(ೣసೣ೐) ∑ ௥౐೙ా೙(௫ୀ௫೐) య ೙సభ ’ (6) where the bond formaƟon transiƟon rate is measured at the eclipsed conﬁguraƟon, 𝑥௘, where it is maximal. In region 3, the binding probability depends on the sliding history. If in region 2 the bond TଵBଵ is formed (and eventually ruptures), healing consistently occurs by a transfer of an atom between the layers, yielding a transiƟon from a V1V1 to a V0V2 conﬁguraƟon (prisƟne 7 boƩom layer and a double vacancy in the top layer). In such a case, interlayer bonding in region 3 is prevented. Hence, the probability for bond formaƟon in this region is given by: 𝑝୘భ୆మ(𝜎, 𝑣, 𝑇) = ൫1 −𝑝୘భ୆భ൯൫1 −𝑠ଷ(𝑥ଷ ௠௔௫, 𝜎, 𝑣, 𝑇)൯, (7) where 𝑠ଷ is given by Eq. 3 with 𝐸௠௜௡= 𝐸௠௜௡் భ஻మ being the load-free energy barrier for 𝑇ଵ𝐵ଶ bond formaƟon in this region. In our calculaƟons we chose 𝑓଴= 100 nsିଵ(49), and set 𝑥௘ to be 1.52, 1.73, and 1.97 nm, corresponding to the eclipsed dangling atoms conﬁguraƟons in each region, respecƟvely. The values of the rest of the kineƟc model parameters are manually ﬁt to obtain good agreement with our atomisƟc simulaƟon results (see Fig. 2 and SI Fig. S4). The ﬁƩed values are 𝛼= 0.048 eV/GPa , 𝛽= 2.500 eV/nmଶ , and 𝐸୫୧୬= 0.185 , 0.125 , 0.110 , 0.108 , and 0.220 eV, for the TଶBଵ, TଵBଵ, TଶBଶ, TଷBଷ, and TଵBଶ bond pairs, respecƟvely. These values agree well with those predicted by nudged elasƟc band (NEB) calculaƟons (see SI Table S1). The chosen values for 𝛼 and 𝛽 were further validated through zero temperature reacƟve sliding dynamics as detailed in SI secƟon S4. One can now use the developed phenomenological model to evaluate the interlayer bonding probability at experimentally relevant sliding velociƟes. As can be seen in Fig. 2c and SI Fig. S4c, at the low sliding velocity regime the binding probability saturates in all three regions. Speciﬁcally, under a normal load of 2 GPa , 𝑝ଵ and 𝑝ଶ reach the maximal value of 1 below sliding velociƟes of ~0.1 m s ⁄ and ~5 m s ⁄ , respecƟvely, whereas 𝑝ଷ saturates at 0.8 below a sliding velocity of ~0.01 m s ⁄ . We note that the laƩer does not reach the maximal value of 1 due to the healing process discussed above, suggesƟng that when a TଵBଵ pair forms in region 2, binding in region 3 is inhibited. As shown in SI Fig. S5, our model predicts that the saturaƟon velocity decreases exponenƟally with reducƟon of the normal load. Since fricƟon in our system is dominated by interlayer chemical bonding, one can use the bonding probability funcƟons derived above to esƟmate the dependence of the fricƟonal shear stress on external parameters, such as the normal load and the sliding velocity. Marking by 𝑓୘೔୆ೕ(𝑥) the shear stress trace associated with the T௜B௝ bonding event (𝑖 and 𝑗 represenƟng dangling atom indices in the top and boƩom layers, respecƟvely), the overall shear stress trace can be wriƩen as the following sum: 𝜏(𝑥) = 𝑝୘మ୆భ𝑓୘మ୆భ(𝑥) + ∑ 𝑝୘೙୆೙𝑓୘೙୆೙(𝑥) ଷ ௡ୀଵ + 𝑝୘భ୆మ𝑓୘భ୆మ(𝑥). (8) Here, based on the atomisƟc simulaƟon results, we assume that binding events in diﬀerent regions are spaƟally separated and that in region 2 only a single binding event per trace can occur. The individual shear stress traces can be approximated using harmonic springs of eﬀecƟve sƟﬀness, 𝑘ୣ୤୤ ୘೔୆ೕ, spanning from the iniƟal bond formaƟon posiƟon, 𝑥௕ ୘೔୆ೕ, to the rupture posiƟon, 𝑥௕ ୘೔୆ೕ+ 𝑙௥ ୘೔୆ೕ(𝑇, 𝑣), yielding: 8 𝑓୘೔୆ೕ(𝑥) = ൝ ௞౛౜౜ ౐೔ాೕ ஺ೞቀ𝑥−𝑥௕ ୘೔୆ೕቁ 0 for 𝑥௕ ୘೔୆ೕ≤𝑥≤𝑥௕ ୘೔୆ೕ+ 𝑙௥ ୘೔୆ೕ(𝑇, 𝑣) for 𝑥< 𝑥௕ ୘೔୆ೕ 𝑜𝑟 𝑥> 𝑥௕ ୘೔୆ೕ+ 𝑙௥ ୘೔୆ೕ(𝑇, 𝑣) , (9) where 𝐴௦ is the in-plane surface area. Here, the bond formaƟon posiƟon and spring sƟﬀnesses are assumed to be constant, whereas the bond rupture lengths, 𝑙௥(𝑇, 𝑣), are assumed to depend only on the sliding velocity and the temperature (50-54). 𝑙௥ can be evaluated by the surface area encompassed by the bond survival probability distribuƟon across the sliding displacement. This probability can be esƟmated via the same kineƟc model used above (Eqs. 1-2) for bond formaƟon, where the bond rupture energy barrier in the ﬁnite harmonic potenƟal well decreases quadraƟcally with lateral interlayer displacement: 𝐸(𝑥) = 𝐸௥ ௠௔௫− ଵ ଶ𝑘ୣ୤୤ ୘೔୆ೕቀ𝑥−𝑥௕ ୘೔୆ೕቁ ଶ , (10) and −𝐸௥ ௠௔௫ is the harmonic potenƟal minimum. SubsƟtuƟng Eq. 10 in Eq. 1 and solving for Eq. 2, one obtains the bond survival probability (55, 56): 𝑠௥(𝑥, 𝑣, 𝑇) = 𝑒 ష√ഏ೑బ మೡටೖ౛౜౜/൫మೖా ೅൯ ௘ షಶೝ೘ೌೣ ೖా೅ୣ ୰୤୧ቀඥ௞౛౜౜/(ଶ௞ా் )(௫ି௫್)ቁ , (11) where erϐi(𝑥) = ଶ √గ∫𝑒௧మ𝑑𝑡 ௫ ଴ is the imaginary error funcƟon. This allows us to evaluate the probability density funcƟon for bond rupture at a displacement interval between 𝑥 and 𝑥+ 𝑑𝑥 as − డ௦ೝ(௫,௩,்) డ௫ 𝑑𝑥. Averaging the bond rupture length over this probability density funcƟon yields the mean bond rupture length: 𝑙௥ ୘೔୆ೕ(𝑇, 𝑣) = ∫ 𝑑𝑥ቀ𝑥−𝑥௕ ୘೔୆ೕቁ ஶ ௫್ ౐೔ాೕ ቂ− డ௦ೝ(௫,௩,்) డ௫ ቃ= ∫ 𝑑𝑥 𝑠௥(𝑥, 𝑣, 𝑇) ஶ ௫್ ౐೔ాೕ , (12) where we have used integraƟon by parts and assumed that 𝑠௥(𝑥, 𝑣, 𝑇) vanishes at 𝑥→∞. This allows us to evaluate the shear stress traces, 𝑓୘೔୆ೕ(𝑥), of Eq. 9 with the parameters extracted from the reacƟve sliding dynamics simulaƟons (see SI SecƟon S6 for details), which together with Eqs. 4-7 for the bond formaƟon probabiliƟes, 𝑝୘೔୆ೕ(𝜎, 𝑣, 𝑇), provide an esƟmaƟon of the overall fricƟonal shear stress traces via Eq. 8. 9 Fig. 3. Normal load (a) and velocity (b) dependence of the chemical contribuƟon to the kineƟc fricƟonal stress of a twisted V1V1 bilayer at a temperature of 300 K. Symbols represent atomisƟc simulaƟon results and lines represent model predicƟons (Eq. 8). Error bars for MD results are calculated from the standard error of the mean across ensembles of 100 trajectories for 10 m s ⁄ sliding velocity results and 50 trajectories for 5 m s ⁄ results. To isolate the chemifricƟon eﬀect, we subtract from the atomisƟc simulaƟon results the (nearly constant) fricƟonal stress obtained for the prisƟne V0V0 bilayer (~0.008 GPa, see Fig. 5c of Ref. (34)), which is not considered by our phenomenological model. The derived phenomenological model reproduces the shear stress traces obtained from the fully atomisƟc simulaƟon in a broad range of normal loads with remarkable success (see Fig. 1b). Given the reliability of the model and the fact that all its assumpƟons and parameters should hold also outside the condiƟons characterizing our simulaƟon results, we may now use it to predict chemifricƟonal eﬀects in experimentally accessible sliding velociƟes (10, 57). Figure 3 shows the mean kineƟc fricƟonal stress dependence on the normal load (Fig. 3a) and sliding velocity (Fig. 3b) under such condiƟons. As expected, at the high-velocity regime there is a monotonic increase of fricƟon with normal load, due to the corresponding increase of bond formaƟon probability (see Fig. 2a and SI Fig. S4a). Notably, at low velociƟes the fricƟon is found to be nearly independent of the applied load. This is aƩributed to the fact that the bond formaƟon probability itself becomes independent of normal load at this experimentally relevant regime (see SI Fig. S6). For the velocity dependence of fricƟon, we ﬁnd two disƟnct regimes. At experimentally relevant velociƟes fricƟon increases logarithmically. This can be raƟonalized by the fact that the bond rupture length increases with sliding velocity (55, 56), because thermal ﬂuctuaƟons do not have suﬃcient Ɵme to assist the bond rupture barrier crossing. A turnover is predicted to occur at higher velociƟes, where fricƟon is found to drasƟcally decrease with sliding velocity, due to reduced probability of interlayer bond formaƟon. Notably, at the high velocity regime, both the load and the velocity dependence of the fricƟonal stress exhibit a wavy structure. This is aƩributed to the diﬀerence in the TEBs associated with various interlayer bonding pairs that are acƟvated at diﬀerent velociƟes and normal loads. 10 Fig. 4. Sliding induced healing. (a) NEB results for two reacƟon pathways of TଵBଵ bond rupture, one leading to the V1V1 conﬁguraƟon (orange) and the other to the V0V2 conﬁguraƟon (blue). Symbols and solid lines represent the energies of individual images and a cubic polynomial interpolaƟon, respecƟvely. (b) FricƟonal stress traces of the V0V0 (green), V1V1 (orange), and V0V2 (blue) conﬁguraƟons under normal loads of 1 (top sub-panel), 2 (middle sub-panel), and 3 (boƩom sub-panel) GPa. (c) Average fricƟonal stress calculated for the three conﬁguraƟons as a funcƟon of normal load obtained from traces such as those presented in panel (b). As menƟoned earlier, a unique sliding induced healing process occurs when the TଵBଵ pair bond ruptures, involving the transfer of the Tଵ atom from the top layer to the boƩom layer. This results in a V0V2 conﬁguraƟon, where the boƩom layer is prisƟne, and the top layer includes a double vacancy (see the inset of Fig. 4a and SI Movie S4). NEB calculaƟons reveal that the TEB for this healing transiƟon (0.67 eV) is signiﬁcantly lower than that for reverƟng back to the original V1V1 conﬁguraƟon (1.74 eV ) upon bond rupture (see SI secƟon S2 for computaƟonal details). The resulƟng V0V2 conﬁguraƟon presents smooth superlubric moƟon, similar to that of the prisƟne V0V0 bilayer for all normal loads considered (see Fig. 4b). At a temperature of 𝑇= 300 K and a sliding velocity of 𝑣= 10 m/s, the coeﬃcient of fricƟon of the V0V2 conﬁguraƟon is 3.2 × 10-4, compared to 3.8 × 10-4 for the prisƟne bilayer and 6.3 × 10-3 for the V1V1 conﬁguraƟon, as shown in Fig. 4c. Since the healing process is iniƟated above a normal load of ~0.5 GPa, we expect a reducƟon of fricƟon with increasing normal load around this threshold, resulƟng in a negaƟve diﬀerenƟal fricƟon coeﬃcient. The revealed sliding-induced healing process can serve to consƟtute a run-in procedure, where pre-sliding cycles reduce fricƟon. To demonstrate this, we conducted repeated forward and backward sliding simulaƟons for the twisted V1V1 bilayer at 𝑇 = 300 K and 𝑣= 10 m/s . To accelerate the simulaƟons, the maximal lateral distance between two vacancies was set at 0.74 nm, corresponding to a separaƟon of three intra-layer laƫce vectors. Each trajectory consisted of 20 cycles, with a forward and backward sliding distance of 1.5 nm each. KineƟc fricƟonal stress results were averaged over 30 independent trajectories for 𝜎= 0.5 GPa, and 10 trajectories for 𝜎= 1.0 and 2.0 GPa. 11 At a normal load of 𝜎= 1.0 GPa and above, we ﬁnd a sharp drop in the corrugaƟon of the average fricƟon stress traces aŌer a few cycles (< 5, see Fig. 5a), corresponding to a transiƟon from the V1V1 to the V0V2 conﬁguraƟon (manifested as a reducƟon in the survival probability of the V1V1 conﬁguraƟon and overall trace averaged fricƟonal stress, see Fig. 5b). Notably, Fig. 5b also shows that the healing transiƟon is accelerated under higher normal loads, due to the corresponding increase in the TଵBଵ pair interlayer bond formaƟon probability in region 2 (see Fig. 2a). These results thus demonstrate that the suggested run-in process leads to a healing-induced transiƟon to a superlubric state, reminiscent of that of the prisƟne interface, which can be further enhanced by increasing the normal load. Fig. 5. SimulaƟons of the run-in procedure. (a) Averaged fricƟon stress traces (blue and orange lines represent the trace and retrace, respecƟvely) obtained for nine sliding simulaƟon cycles of a twisted bilayer starƟng in a V1V1 conﬁguraƟon under an external normal load of 1.0 GPa. Here, averaging is performed over 10 independent trajectories. The shaded regions mark the standard error of the mean. (b) The evoluƟon of the fricƟonal stress (main panel, averaged over the enƟre average trajectory) and the survival probability of the V1V1 conﬁguraƟon (inset, 𝑝௏భ௏భ) as a funcƟon of cycle number, calculated under external normal loads of 0.5 (red), 1.0 (purple), and 2.0 (brown) GPa. Summary and conclusions In summary, using a recently developed machine learning potenƟal trained against ab- iniƟo reference data, we conducted reacƟve sliding dynamics simulaƟons of a twisted incommensurate defected bilayer graphene interface. Our results reveal a unique microscopic interlayer bonding mechanism underlying chemifricƟon that may impede superlubric sliding. This mechanism involves consecuƟve stochasƟc bond formaƟon and rupture events, that are spaƟally separated but not necessarily independent. Notably, an intricate healing process, involving interlayer atomic transfer, was idenƟﬁed that leads to the occurrence of negaƟve diﬀerenƟal fricƟon coeﬃcients and may be harnessed to design a fricƟon reducƟon run-in procedure. Leveraging the derived atomisƟc insights, we developed a physically moƟvated phenomenological model that illustrates the interplay 12 between bond formaƟon kineƟcs, which are predominantly acƟvated at high sliding velociƟes, and rupture kineƟcs, which become signiﬁcant at lower velociƟes. This model allows us to predict chemifricƟonal eﬀects in the experimentally accessible low sliding velocity regime, revealing a disƟnct transiƟon from a logarithmic increase to a logarithmic decrease of fricƟonal stress with velocity. While one may naively assume that chemifricƟon would always increase fricƟon, our results demonstrate that under appropriate condiƟons, it may actually lead to the reducƟon of fricƟon and support superlubric sliding. Acknowledgements P.Y. is supported by the Israel Academy of Sciences and HumaniƟes & Council for Higher EducaƟon Excellence Fellowship Program for InternaƟonal Postdoctoral Researchers. O.H. is grateful for the generous ﬁnancial support of the Heineman Chair in Physical Chemistry. M.U. is grateful for generous ﬁnancial support via the BSF-NSF 2023614 grant. M.U. and O.H acknowledge computaƟonal support by the Tel Aviv University Center for Nanoscience and Nanotechnology. References 1. K. Shinjo, M. Hirano, Dynamics of FricƟon - Superlubric State. Surface Science 283, 473-478 (1993). 2. O. Hod, QuanƟfying the Stacking Registry Matching in Layered Materials. Israel Journal of Chemistry 50, 506- 514 (2010). 3. M. H. Müser, Structural lubricity: Role of dimension and symmetry. Europhysics LeƩers (EPL) 66, 97-103 (2004). 4. D. Berman, A. Erdemir, A. V. Sumant, Graph